  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nWe'll implement a two-step strategy combining global search with local optimization:\n1. First perform a global search over a coarse grid using the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) and selective pruning of non-promising regions\n2. Then apply local optimization (gradient descent with momentum) to refine the champion candidate\n\nThis approach improves upon the previous method by:\n- Using FFT to enable much finer grid resolution (1024 points) within time budget\n- Identifying areas of high correlation and focusing optimization there\n- Maintaining feasibility through sophisticated projection methods\n\nMathematical Specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise linear function interpolated from a grid.\nThe optimization domain is [0,2] with integral constraint sum(h) = 1024.\n\nKey ingredients:\n1. The objective is defined as:\n   C_max = max_{shifts} \u222b h(x) \u00b7 (1-h(x+shift)) dx\n2. We must enforce:\n   0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n   \u222b_{0}^{2} h(x) dx = 1\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Global pruning:\n   - Initial coarse grid evaluation\n   - Use Wigner function to estimate cross-correlation errors\n   - Select promising regions based on minimal estimated overlap\n\n2. Local refinement:\n   - Start with interpolated candidate from pruned region\n   - Perform projected gradient descent with adaptive momentum\n   - Use integral-preserving projection scheme\n\nAlgorithm details will be specified by coder, including:\n- Grid generation and feasibility restoration\n- FFT-based correlation computation\n- Wigner-based pruning criteria\n- Adjoint gradients for projected optimization\n\nSearch choices:\n- Small-scale (64-point) initialization\n- Medium-scale (256-point) pruning\n- Final 1024-point optimization\n- Time allocation: 30% pruning, 70% optimization\n\nNumerical Considerations:\n- Use periodic boundary handling with projections\n- Integrate via trapezoidal rule\n- Maintain floating-point stability in correlation computations\n- Ensure integral conservation during interpolation\n\nRuntimesafeguards:\n- Strict time tracking via `time_left()`\n- Memory-efficient array operations\n- Best candidate preserved throughout optimization\n- Automatic fallback to feasible solution if optimization fails",
